# Introduction

Financial returns show volatility clustering: calm and turbulent periods tend to persist
[@engle1982; @bollerslev1986]. For equity indices, negative returns also tend to raise volatility
more than positive returns of the same size, the leverage effect [@black1976], which the exponential
GARCH (EGARCH) model of @nelson1991 accommodates directly. Both models are widely used to forecast
volatility and Value-at-Risk (VaR), usually with fat-tailed innovations [@bollerslev1987].

Two literatures frame the questions asked here. On forecasting, surveys report that option-implied
volatility is often the most informative predictor of future realized volatility [@poon2003], and
@christensen1998 show with encompassing regressions that implied volatility from S&P 100 options
contains information that past volatility does not. @hansen2005 compare hundreds of models and find that
a GARCH(1,1) is hard to beat on exchange rates but is outperformed on equity returns by models with a
leverage effect. Implied variance also tends to exceed subsequently realized variance, a variance risk
premium [@carr2009; @bollerslev2009]. On risk, VaR forecasts are judged by the coverage and independence
of their exceedances [@kupiec1995; @christoffersen1998; @engle2004] and expected shortfall (ES) by the
losses beyond VaR [@mcneil2000]; filtered historical simulation is a standard way to relax the
distributional assumption [@barone1999].

This report brings these strands together in one reproducible pipeline for the CAC 40 and the EURO STOXX 50,
with VSTOXX as the implied volatility benchmark. It asks four questions:

1. Is there a leverage effect, and does an asymmetric model (EGARCH) fit better than GARCH?
2. Do the models forecast realized volatility as well as the market's implied volatility (VSTOXX)?
3. Do the models contain information that implied volatility does not (encompassing regressions)?
4. Are one-day VaR and Expected Shortfall forecasts well calibrated, and what fixes under-coverage?

In short, EGARCH is preferred in-sample, but its leverage term does not translate into significantly
better 21-day volatility forecasts; VSTOXX tracks realized volatility better than either model and
encompasses GARCH; and in this sample the tail specification matters more for one-day VaR calibration than
the choice between GARCH and EGARCH. Model selection in the VaR experiment follows a rule fixed in advance
and is evaluated on a hold-out period. The remaining sections describe the data and methods, report the
results, and discuss limitations.

# Data

- CAC 40 (`^FCHI`) adjusted close from Yahoo Finance. The in-sample analysis and the one-day evaluation use
  2015-01-05 to 2026-10-26 (3,023 returns); the VSTOXX comparison and the VaR work have forecast origins from
  2008 and use data from 2000 for the first estimation windows.
- EURO STOXX 50 (`^STOXX50E`), Yahoo Finance, from 2007-04-02.
- VSTOXX (V2TX) daily closes from STOXX, 1999-2026.

Missing prices are dropped, not interpolated. CAC 40 returns (2015-2026) have skewness -0.83 and excess
kurtosis 11.19 (Jarque-Bera p < 2.2e-16); the Ljung-Box test on squared returns rejects at all lags tested
(Q(10) = 1,144.9) and the ARCH-LM test rejects at lags 5 and 10.

With a 1,000-day estimation window, the first forecast origin for the EURO STOXX 50 is 2011-03-23, whereas
the CAC 40 supports origins from 2008-01-02. The CAC 40 is therefore the only index covering 2008-2010
in the comparison with VSTOXX, which is an implied volatility on the EURO STOXX 50. Daily returns of the two
indices correlate at 0.969 over 2007-2026 (0.976 in 2008-2009 and 0.987 in 2020), which supports its use
as a proxy but does not remove the mismatch.

