# Methods

## Models

With $r_t = \mu + \varepsilon_t$, $\varepsilon_t = \sigma_t z_t$ and $z_t$ standardized Student-t:

$$\sigma_t^2 = \omega + \alpha\,\varepsilon_{t-1}^2 + \beta\,\sigma_{t-1}^2 \qquad \text{(GARCH)}$$

$$\ln\sigma_t^2 = \omega + \beta\ln\sigma_{t-1}^2 + \alpha_1 z_{t-1} + \gamma_1\left(|z_{t-1}| - E|z|\right) \qquad \text{(EGARCH, rugarch notation)}$$

Models are estimated by maximum likelihood with the R package `rugarch`, using robust standard errors.
In `rugarch`, $\alpha_1$ is the sign (leverage) coefficient and $\gamma_1$ the magnitude coefficient; a
negative $\alpha_1$ means negative shocks raise volatility more than positive ones. The tail experiment also
uses the skewed Student-t distribution of `rugarch`, which follows @fernandez1998.

## Out-of-sample design

Rolling estimation with a moving window of 1,000 days (2,000 days in the tail experiment), parameters
re-estimated every 50 days. One-day variance forecasts are scored with QLIKE and squared-error losses,
both robust to noise in the variance proxy [@patton2011], and compared with Diebold-Mariano tests
[@diebold1995]. Where forecast windows overlap, variances are Newey-West (HAC) estimates [@newey1987].

## Comparison with implied volatility

VSTOXX is a 30-calendar-day implied volatility, so model forecasts are averaged over the next 21 trading
days. Realized volatility is measured over the 21 trading days after each forecast origin. The variance
risk premium is VSTOXX minus subsequent realized volatility. Encompassing regressions [@christensen1998]
regress realized volatility on VSTOXX and a model forecast, with Newey-West standard errors (lag 20, with
lag 40 as a check). To separate tracking from bias, losses are also computed after removing each series'
full-sample mean level; this is a diagnostic, not a tradable forecast.

## VaR and Expected Shortfall

One-day 1%, 2.5% and 5% VaR and ES. Tests: Kupiec (coverage) [@kupiec1995], Christoffersen (independence
and conditional coverage) [@christoffersen1998], the dynamic quantile test [@engle2004], and a McNeil-Frey
exceedance-residual test for ES [@mcneil2000], plus tick loss with Diebold-Mariano tests. A tail experiment
varies the mean (constant or zero), the distribution (Student-t or skewed Student-t), the window
(1,000 or 2,000 days) and the tail estimate (parametric, or filtered historical simulation
[@barone1999]). Candidates are ranked on 2008-2016 by the mean of |log(exceedance rate / nominal level)|
at 1% and 5%, a rule fixed before running, and reported on 2017-2026, which is not used for selection.

