kable(e, digits = 4, booktabs = TRUE)
```

```{r}
#| label: tbl-fit
#| tbl-cap: "In-sample model comparison and residual diagnostics (p-values), CAC 40 2015-2026."
# Typed from the console output of R/03_fits.R
fit_tab <- data.frame(
  Measure = c("Log-likelihood", "AIC (per observation)", "BIC (per observation)",
              "Persistence (GARCH: alpha + beta; EGARCH: beta)", "Half-life of a shock (days)",
              "Ljung-Box on z, lag 10 / 20", "Ljung-Box on z squared, lag 10 / 20",
              "ARCH-LM on z, q = 5 / 10"),
  GARCH  = c("9,766.1", "-6.4579", "-6.4479", "0.9725", "24.9",
             "0.985 / 0.876", "0.515 / 0.847", "0.255 / 0.577"),
  EGARCH = c("9,836.2", "-6.5036", "-6.4916", "0.9692", "22.2",
             "0.957 / 0.786", "0.916 / 0.993", "0.653 / 0.931"))
kable(fit_tab, booktabs = TRUE, align = "lrr")
```

EGARCH is preferred by AIC, BIC and log-likelihood (@tbl-fit). Its sign coefficient $\alpha_1$ is negative
and highly significant (@tbl-egarch): a one-unit negative standardized shock raises log-variance by about
0.30, whereas a one-unit positive shock lowers it by about 0.04 (@fig-nic). Standardized residuals of both
models show no remaining autocorrelation or ARCH effects. For EGARCH, the sign bias test (joint p = 0.46) and
the Nyblom stability test (joint statistic 1.25, below the 10% critical value of 1.49) raise no concern, but
the adjusted Pearson goodness-of-fit test rejects the Student-t distribution (p < 0.01). The GARCH
intercept $\omega$ is not significant (p = 0.52), so its implied long-run volatility (about 20% annualized)
is imprecise. The extremely small standard error on the EGARCH $\beta$ is an optimizer artifact: $\omega$ and
$\beta$ are nearly collinear in the log-variance recursion.

