## Encompassing regressions

```{r}
#| label: tbl-enc
#| tbl-cap: "Regressions of realized 21-day volatility on VSTOXX and model forecasts (levels unless stated; coefficient with Newey-West lag-20 t-statistic in parentheses)."
enc <- read.csv(out("encompassing_regressions.csv"))
g1 <- function(ds, sp, tm, col, sc = "vol")
  enc[enc$dataset == ds & enc$scale == sc & enc$spec == sp & enc$term == tm, col]
ct <- function(ds, sp, tm, sc = "vol")
  sprintf("%+.3f (%+.2f)", g1(ds, sp, tm, "est", sc), g1(ds, sp, tm, "t20", sc))
enc_col <- function(ds) c(
  fx(g1(ds, "1: implied", "implied", "r2"), 3),
  fx(g1(ds, "2: GARCH", "garch", "r2"), 3),
  fx(g1(ds, "3: EGARCH", "egarch", "r2"), 3),
  fx(g1(ds, "4: implied + GARCH", "garch", "r2"), 3),
  fx(g1(ds, "5: implied + EGARCH", "egarch", "r2"), 3),
  ct(ds, "4: implied + GARCH", "garch"),
  ct(ds, "5: implied + EGARCH", "egarch"),
  ct(ds, "4: implied + GARCH", "implied"),
  ct(ds, "5: implied + EGARCH", "implied"),
  sprintf("%.3f (%+.2f)", g1(ds, "1: implied", "implied", "est"),
          (g1(ds, "1: implied", "implied", "est") - 1) / g1(ds, "1: implied", "implied", "se20")),
  ct(ds, "4: implied + GARCH", "garch", "log"),
  ct(ds, "5: implied + EGARCH", "egarch", "log"))
enc_labels <- c("R-squared: VSTOXX alone", "R-squared: GARCH alone", "R-squared: EGARCH alone",
                "R-squared: VSTOXX + GARCH", "R-squared: VSTOXX + EGARCH",
                "GARCH coefficient in VSTOXX + GARCH", "EGARCH coefficient in VSTOXX + EGARCH",
                "VSTOXX coefficient in VSTOXX + GARCH", "VSTOXX coefficient in VSTOXX + EGARCH",
                "Slope on VSTOXX alone (t-test of slope = 1)",
                "GARCH coefficient, log specification", "EGARCH coefficient, log specification")
kable(data.frame(Measure = enc_labels, `EURO STOXX 50` = enc_col("STOXX50E"),
                 `CAC 40 (proxy)` = enc_col("CAC40"), check.names = FALSE),
      booktabs = TRUE, align = "lrr")
```

VSTOXX remains highly significant when a model forecast is added (t above 8.7 in every joint regression),
while the GARCH coefficient is indistinguishable from zero in both runs and in logs (@tbl-enc).
Adding GARCH changes the R-squared by at most 0.001. The EGARCH coefficient is positive in all four
regressions (0.10 to 0.19) but significant only in the CAC 40 log specification (t = 2.18, and 2.00 with a
lag-40 correction), and the R-squared gain is at most 0.005; this is weak evidence at most. In the
sub-periods, no model coefficient is significant for the CAC 40; in 2011-2012 on the EURO STOXX 50 the GARCH
coefficient is 0.63 (t = 2.29), but that sample has about 21 independent windows.

In levels, the regression on VSTOXX alone is close to realized = 0.8 x VSTOXX, with an intercept near zero
(0.10 and 1.09 vol points, neither significant) and a slope significantly below one; in logs the slope is
0.94 to 0.98. The premium therefore looks proportional to the volatility level and not a constant number
of points. A slope below one can also arise from noise in the regressor, which these data cannot separate.

