## VaR and Expected Shortfall

```{r}
#| label: tbl-var
#| tbl-cap: "One-day VaR backtest, CAC 40, 4,812 days (2008-2026). Student-t models with constant mean and a 1,000-day window; HS250 = 250-day historical simulation. ES p = one-sided p-value that losses beyond VaR exceed the forecast ES."
v <- read.csv(out("var_backtest_CAC40.csv"))
v <- v[order(v$alpha), ]
kable(data.frame(Level = sprintf("%.0f%%", 100 * v$alpha), Model = v$model,
                 `Exceed.` = v$exceed, `Rate (%)` = fx(v$rate_pct, 2),
                 `Kupiec p` = pf(v$kupiec_p), `Indep. p` = pf(v$indep_p),
                 `Cond. cov. p` = pf(v$cc_p), `ES p` = pf(v$es_p_less),
                 `Tick loss (x1e4)` = fx(v$tick_x1e4, 2), check.names = FALSE),
      booktabs = TRUE, row.names = FALSE, align = "llrrrrrrr")
```

Over 2008-2026 the 1% VaR was breached on 1.48% (GARCH), 1.81% (EGARCH) and 1.52% (HS250) of days against
a nominal 1% (48.1 expected exceedances in 4,812 days), and the Kupiec test rejects for all three
(@tbl-var). Both GARCH models have lower tick loss than historical simulation (Diebold-Mariano p at most
0.001) but are not distinguishable from each other. The GARCH ES passes the McNeil-Frey test at 1% (p = 0.46),
while historical simulation underestimates tail losses (p = 0.002). EGARCH is the worst on coverage: 2008 and
2020 contribute 26 of its 87 exceedances, but outside those two years its rate is still 1.42% (GARCH: 1.35%).

```{r}
#| label: tbl-exp
#| tbl-cap: "Tail experiment, hold-out 2017-2026 (2,512 days; not used for selection). DQ p = dynamic quantile test; ES p as in the previous table."
a <- read.csv(out("var_experiments_all.csv"))
lab <- c("sGARCH_mC_std_W1000|param"  = "Baseline: GARCH, const. mean, Student-t, 1,000 d",
         "sGARCH_m0_sstd_W1000|param" = "Selected: GARCH, zero mean, skewed-t, 1,000 d",
         "eGARCH_mC_sstd_W2000|param" = "EGARCH, const. mean, skewed-t, 2,000 d",
         "eGARCH_m0_sstd_W2000|param" = "EGARCH, zero mean, skewed-t, 2,000 d",
         "HS250"                      = "Historical simulation, 250 d")
h <- a[a$period == "hold-out 2017-2026" & a$candidate %in% names(lab), ]
h$spec <- factor(unname(lab[h$candidate]), levels = unname(lab))
h <- h[order(h$alpha, h$spec), ]
kable(data.frame(Level = sprintf("%.1f%%", 100 * h$alpha), Specification = as.character(h$spec),
                 `Exceed.` = h$exceed, `Rate (%)` = fx(h$rate_pct, 2), `Kupiec p` = pf(h$kupiec_p),
                 `DQ p` = pf(h$dq_p), `ES p` = pf(h$es_p_less),
                 `Tick loss (x1e4)` = fx(h$tick_x1e4, 2), check.names = FALSE),
      booktabs = TRUE, row.names = FALSE, align = "llrrrrrr")
```

The baseline under-covers on the hold-out as well: 1.55% of days breach the 1% VaR (39 against 25.1 expected;
Kupiec p = 0.010). Sixteen specifications (GARCH or EGARCH, constant or zero mean, Student-t or skewed
Student-t, 1,000 or 2,000 days) were each combined with a parametric and a filtered historical simulation tail,
giving 33 candidates with the historical simulation benchmark. The baseline ranks 30th of 33 on 2008-2016. The two
top candidates tied on the selection score; the tie was broken by listing order, so the selected model is the
zero-mean skewed-t GARCH, and the tied EGARCH with a 2,000-day window is reported alongside it (@tbl-exp).

On the hold-out, the skewed-t specifications bring the exceedance rate to 1.04-1.15% at 1% (Kupiec p of 0.44
or more) and pass the ES test at all three levels (p above 0.2), while historical simulation fails it
(p = 0.042, 0.011 and 0.035 at 1%, 2.5% and 5%). For GARCH with a 1,000-day window, zero mean alone cuts
the 39 exceedances to 34, the skewed-t alone to 30, the empirical residual tail (filtered historical
simulation) alone to 26, and zero mean with skewed-t also to 26. Each step changes only a few exceedances, with
sampling noise of about five, so only the baseline against the combined fixes is a visible gap.

Matching the average rate does not make the exceedances unpredictable. The selected GARCH fails the dynamic
quantile test at 1% (p = 0.014) and its exceedances are clustered (independence p = 0.029), whereas the EGARCH
with a 2,000-day window passes (p = 0.41). Among constant-mean skewed-t models at 1%, only GARCH with a
1,000-day window fails (p = 6.6e-4); EGARCH with 1,000 days (0.27), GARCH with 2,000 days (0.45) and EGARCH
with 2,000 days (0.41) pass, with 26 to 31 exceedances each. Either the asymmetry or the longer window is
therefore enough, and the two effects cannot be separated here. In the two crisis years, the EGARCH with a
2,000-day window has 2 (2008) and 7 (2020) exceedances of the 1% VaR, against 15 and 11 for the Student-t
EGARCH of the baseline. Its tick loss at 1% is lower (3.71 against 3.86), which I did not test for significance.

