# Limitations

- 21-day windows overlap: there are about 185 to 227 independent windows per run, so HAC tests may over-reject;
  the lag-40 check agrees with lag 20 almost everywhere.
- The like-for-like EURO STOXX 50 run starts in March 2011, so 2008-2010 is covered only by the CAC 40 proxy.
- Squared returns are a noisy variance proxy and no intraday realized variance is used.
- Implied volatility is risk-neutral, whereas GARCH is estimated under the physical measure, so the two are
  not directly interchangeable.
- Each crisis regime is one or a few episodes; the sub-period regressions are descriptive.
- 33 VaR candidates were evaluated, the selection tie was broken by listing order, and the hold-out has
  about 25 expected exceedances at 1%, so coverage tests have limited power. Many p-values are reported
  without multiple-testing adjustment.
- Parametric ES values assume the fitted distribution; the Student-t is rejected by a goodness-of-fit test and
  the skewed-t was not tested in-sample.
- Mean-removed losses use full-sample means.

# Conclusion

EGARCH fits the CAC 40 better than GARCH in-sample, and its sign coefficient confirms a leverage effect
(question 1). Out of sample, that advantage does not carry over to 21-day volatility forecasts: once mean levels
are removed, GARCH and EGARCH are indistinguishable (p = 0.45 and 0.46), and EGARCH's lower raw error comes mainly
from smaller bias (question 2). VSTOXX tracks realized volatility significantly better than either model, exceeds
it by about 3 to 4 vol points on average, and encompasses GARCH, whose coefficient is zero in joint regressions;
EGARCH adds at most a little (question 3). For one-day risk, a Student-t GARCH or EGARCH with a 1,000-day
window under-covers the 1% VaR (1.5% to 1.8% exceedances); a skewed Student-t distribution (with a 1,000- or 2,000-day window) or an empirical residual tail brings the
hold-out rate close to nominal and the ES test is passed, although exceedances can remain predictable unless
the specification also has the asymmetry or the longer window (question 4).
In this sample, the tail specification mattered more for VaR calibration than the choice between GARCH and EGARCH.

Practically, GARCH-type models are useful as a free, well-calibrated risk baseline when the tail is handled
carefully, but the market's implied volatility is the better forecast of realized volatility. Natural extensions
are a VaR built from implied volatility, realized variance from intraday data, and regime-switching models.

